Subject: [PATCH 526/526] [DS4.1] CED replays the prompt's last 128 rows,
 wherever its chunks end

The V4.1 paper defines the decoder's bounded replay as the last 128 tokens
of the prompt, and DeepSeek's engine anchors every prompt chunk there. CED
anchored each chunk at its own end instead: a final chunk of 1-128 rows
kept every row with no floor, so its rows read the previous chunk's kept
rows, computed under that chunk's own floor. Temperature-0 output of those
prompts (prompt length mod the chunk threshold in 1-128, about 128/T of
longer prompts) depended on the threshold, so on the budget and on TP3
against TP4.

replay_rows gives each prefilling request a floor of max(prefix start,
prefill length - 128) and a kept-row limit: every chunk keeps exactly its
rows from the floor on, and a chunk that ends before the floor keeps its
last row, as the nonfinal skip did. The requests kernel replays from
min(floor, first kept row), so that row replays only itself. Decode rows
and prompt-logprob requests keep the prefix start as their floor and their
rows as before. The drafter's context rows are the kept rows and its
windows start at the replay start (0501), so its context is anchored too.

This generalizes the nonfinal skip: no skip policy, alignment contract,
successor check or multimodal exclusion remains. A prompt whose final chunk
has more than 128 rows keeps its final step's rows and replay start, so its
outputs are unchanged. plan_ced now compacts a short chunk the floor cuts
(the first chunk after a prefix hit just before a chunk boundary); the
runner asks the model state (compacts_short_queries) and runs that step
eagerly, as every compacted step is.

Tests: the planning against the scheduler's aligned chunks at four
thresholds, with prefix hits (every replayed row's window, placeholder rows,
draft windows), next-candidate's final step for final chunks over the
window, the eager check, the model state's chunks through the requests
kernel, and the requests kernel against a reference and, for final chunks
and decode rows, against the frozen r6x kernel.
